.TH symcompact 1 "March 28, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
symcompact \- string compaction for executable files
.SH SYNOPSIS
symcompact [ \fBfilename\fP ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
symcompact
reduces the symbol table size of an executable file.  This is done by
removing unnecessary overlay transfer vectors (text symbols beginning with
a tilde).  In a nonoverlaid program there is no need for both the
underscore (_foo) and tilde (~foo) text symbol and only the underscore
form is kept.  For overlaid programs
if the symbol is in the base segment the tilde form is not needed and again
only the underscore form is preserved.  Running \fBsymcompact\fP typically
reduces the kernel symbol table size by 250 or so symbols.
.PP
It is possible to run both \fBsymcompact\fP and \fBstrcompact\fP to
achieve an even higher degree of symbol and string table compaction.  The
normal sequence is to run \fBsymcompact\fP first followed by \fBstrcompact\fP.
If \fBsymcompact\fP runs out of memory it will be necessary to reverse the
order and run \fBsymcompact\fP a second time - see the BUGS note below.
.PP
The user must have write permission to the executable file.
.PP
.B
symcompact
writes to \fIstderr\fP the initial number of symbols, number of excluded 
symbols, number of removed symbols, current number of symbols,
the initial and final string table size, and the maximum program (Data space)
size.
.PP
.B
symcompact
exits 0 if successful, and >0 if an error occurred.
.SH NOTES
.B symcompact
is usually used only with the kernel.
Almost all executables in the system have been processed with
.I strip(1)
and do not have a symbol table.  Running
.B symcompact
on such files is silly.
.PP
The \fIregister\fP local symbol type is removed from the executable
file.  Since the debugger doesn't know how to deal with those symbols
this is not much of a loss and saves quite a bit of space both in the
symbol table and the string table.
.PP
The \fIfilename\fP symbol type is removed from the executable file. It
is not useful to have the name of object files in the symbol table of an
executable.  Nothing in the system except \fBnm(1)\fP knows what to do
with the N_FN symbol type anyhow.
.PP
.B symcompact
should not be run on .o files that will be passed to the linker.  The linker
will need the tilde form of the symbol if an overlaid executable is being
created.
.SH BUGS
This program can partially negate the benefits of \fBstrcompact\fP because
multiple symbols that refer to the same string result in multiple strings
being placed in the string table.  Running \fBstrcompact\fP again after running
this program fixes this problem.
.SH SEE ALSO
strcompact(1), symorder(1)
